window.BC=window.BC||{};window.BC.meta={"CAISO": {"groupLabel": {"CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "COAL": "Coal", "COAL_BIT": "Coal Bituminous", "COAL_PRB": "Coal PRB", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "OTHER_FOSSIL": "Other Fossil", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas"}, "groups": ["COAL", "COAL_PRB", "COAL_BIT", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "zones": ["NP15", "SP15", "ZP26"]}, "ERCOT": {"groupLabel": {"CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "COAL_LIGNITE": "Coal Lignite", "COAL_PRB": "Coal PRB", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "OTHER_FOSSIL": "Other Fossil", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas"}, "groups": ["COAL_LIGNITE", "COAL_PRB", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "zones": ["Houston", "North", "Northeast", "South", "South_Central", "West"]}, "MISO": {"groupLabel": {"CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "COAL_BIT": "Coal Bituminous", "COAL_LIGNITE": "Coal Lignite", "COAL_PRB": "Coal PRB", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "OTHER_FOSSIL": "Other Fossil", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas"}, "groups": ["COAL_LIGNITE", "COAL_PRB", "COAL_BIT", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "zones": ["MISO-East", "MISO-Illinois", "MISO-Indiana", "MISO-Plains", "MISO-South", "MISO-West"]}, "NEISO": {"groupLabel": {"CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "COAL_BIT": "Coal Bituminous", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "OTHER_FOSSIL": "Other Fossil", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas"}, "groups": ["COAL_BIT", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "zones": ["Boston", "Central", "Connecticut", "North"]}, "NYISO": {"groupLabel": {"CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "COAL_BIT": "Coal Bituminous", "COAL_PRB": "Coal PRB", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas"}, "groups": ["COAL_PRB", "COAL_BIT", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "zones": ["Capital_Hudson", "Long_Island", "NYC", "Upstate_West"]}, "PJM": {"groupLabel": {"CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "COAL_BIT": "Coal Bituminous", "COAL_PRB": "Coal PRB", "COAL_WC": "Coal Waste", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "OTHER_FOSSIL": "Other Fossil", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas"}, "groups": ["COAL_PRB", "COAL_BIT", "COAL_WC", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "zones": ["PJM_AEP_Ohio", "PJM_ATSI", "PJM_Central_PA", "PJM_ComEd", "PJM_Dominion", "PJM_EMAAC", "PJM_SWMAAC", "PJM_West_APS"]}};window.BC.manifest=[{"date": "2026-06-22", "definition": "neiso 24 local-band outage regate (NEW KEEPER, supersedes neiso 23). The neiso-23 keeper config (scripts/run_calibration_full.py --iso NEISO --year 2023 2024 2025 --commitment --ct-deployment --hydro-backfill-year 2024 --hydro-eia930-monthly; gas-hub-basis monthly overlay on by default; CHP BTM basis fix in code) re-solved BYTE-FAITHFULLY on the LOCAL (seasonal) net-load outage band \u2014 high_load_mask now uses a centered rolling +/-30d window (commit 152bb99) instead of a single annual percentile, recovering genuine multi-week shoulder maintenance outages. ONLY the outage input changed vs neiso 23 (regenerated data/raw/campd-unit-outages-NEISO.csv, auto picked up). NEAR-NO-OP as predicted by the keeper's dispatch headroom (neiso-23 noted the same for the annual-band re-gate): CC_REGULAR 52.32/56.48/57.75 TWh (neiso-23 52.34/56.54/57.89; |d|<=0.14); system gas volume error -1.5%/-1.4%/+0.2% (neiso-23 -1.3/-1.6/+0.4). LMP demand-wtd model mean 33.93/39.23/68.33 (neiso-23 34.06/39.00/69.60; vs actual DA 36.82/41.47/67.86 -> -7.8%/-5.4%/+0.7%, marginally closer to actual on 2024/2025, ~flat 2023). Hourly hub MAE 15.23/14.11/21.60. TAIL PRESERVED: model hrs>$200 0/0/13 (IDENTICAL to neiso-23; actual 72/43/290) \u2014 summer/winter scarcity neither collapsed nor re-inflated (2025 model max $258.98). The local band is the forecast-defensible exogenous outage input (keys on net load, not price) and produces an equivalent-or-marginally-better calibration; adopted as the keeper-of-record on the correct input. Carries forward all neiso-23/neiso-22 caveats (priced-import seam node required before a forecast run; daily-AGT winter-oil convexity stays rejected).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-neiso-24-local-band.js", "id": "2026-06-22-neiso-24-local-band", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 24 local band", "shorthand": "neiso-24-local-band", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-22", "definition": "neiso 25 fullstop-override outage re-gate (NEW KEEPER, supersedes neiso 24). The neiso-24 keeper config (run_calibration_full.py --iso NEISO --year 2023 2024 2025 --commitment --ct-deployment --hydro-backfill-year 2024 --hydro-eia930-monthly; monthly gas-hub-basis overlay on by default; CHP BTM basis fix in code) re-solved on the SHARED FULL-STOP DURATION OVERRIDE outage input (commits 56ca468/debad10): filter_revealed_outages now keeps a detected down-span if it overlaps >=24 high-net-load hours OR is a sustained full stop (span mean-CF<0.02) for >=14 continuous days, recovering the thin layer of genuinely-mechanical 14-20d weeks-long dead stops the local band still dropped at the 24h overlap margin. Measured-only (CAMPD CF + EIA-930 net load), one shared N=14/depth across all ISOs, no per-ISO constant, no price/MWh-residual (claude.md #11/#12: keep real measured outages). NEISO recovered +39 windows / 15 spans (Merrimack coal, Granite Ridge/Newington/Westbrook/Bellingham CC, Stony Brook, Pittsfield), 0.6% of detected GW-days, all small units; the STEP-1 audit (docs/multi-iso/outage-gate-fullstop-override-2026-06.md) predicted a near-no-op for NEISO and it is. ONLY the outage CSV changed vs neiso 24 (auto picked up). RESULT vs neiso 24: gas tot 54.82/58.92/60.32 TWh vs EIA-930 55.47/59.64/60.09 -> -1.2%/-1.2%/+0.4% (neiso-24 -1.5/-1.4/+0.2, marginally CLOSER to EIA-930 on 2023/24); CC_REGULAR 52.35/56.50/57.88 (neiso-24 52.32/56.48/57.75; |d|<=0.13). LMP model mean 33.96/39.24/68.74 (neiso-24 33.93/39.23/68.33) vs actual DA 36.82/41.51/67.86 -> -7.8%/-5.5%/+1.3%. Hourly hub MAE vs actual RT 15.24/14.10/21.64 (IDENTICAL to neiso-24 15.23/14.11/21.60). TAIL PRESERVED: model hrs>$200 0/0/14 (neiso-24 0/0/13; actual RT 72/43/290), 2025 model max $258.98 unchanged -- the recovered winter full-stops (Granite Ridge/Merrimack Feb 2025) add exactly one marginal 2025 scarcity hour, neither collapsing nor re-inflating the tail. The full-stop override is the structurally-correct forecast-defensible exogenous outage input (keys on net load + measured CF, regenerates forward) and produces an equivalent-or-marginally-better calibration; adopted as keeper-of-record on the correct input. Carries forward all neiso-24/23/22 caveats: priced-import seam node REQUIRED before a forecast run (imports on a fixed EIA-930 schedule); winter oil under-generation (model 0.00/0.00/0.06 vs EIA-930 0.32/0.37/1.24 TWh) is the monthly-AGT-granularity limit and the dominant remaining residual -- the >$200 winter tail (0/0/14 vs 72/43/290) is downstream of it; daily-AGT winter-oil convexity stays REJECTED as a fitted constant (derive from measured daily AGT spot, upload U4).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-neiso-25-fullstop-override.js", "id": "2026-06-22-neiso-25-fullstop-override", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 25 fullstop override", "shorthand": "neiso-25-fullstop-override", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-23", "definition": "neiso 26 ps-diag (DIAGNOSIS, not a calibration keeper). Byte-faithful re-solve of the neiso-25 fullstop keeper config (--commitment --ct-deployment --hydro-backfill-year 2024 --hydro-eia930-monthly; identical offer curves/overlays/outages, scarcity off, PS adder 0) to surface and root-cause the newly-wired C5b storage-throughput metric. FINDING: NEISO PS under-cycles (2025 model 0.59 vs EIA-930 2.08 TWh, -72%) NOT because of a storage defect but because the energy-only LP collapses the scarcity tail (model >$200 hrs 0/0/14 vs actual 72/43/290; 2025 max $258.98 vs ~$1110). PS round-trip breakeven needs an intraday price ratio >1.25 (RTE 0.80); the model's median daily ratio is 1.15, so PS clears only ~50 days/yr (~20 cycles vs the ~107 needed for 2 TWh) -- energy cap (18.6 GWh) and the $0 adder are non-binding. Step-1 measurement audit: 2023 EIA-930 PS is fully blank and 2024 begins only in Nov (14.8% coverage), so the old +47% '2024 over-cycle' was a 2-month partial scored as a full year; fixed by a coverage gate in eia_loader (storage series <50% reported -> SKIPPED). 2025 under-cycle is a real MODEL MISS handed to the scarcity/winter-gas workstream (daily AGT spot U4 / NEISO reserve scarcity pricing) -- NO storage knob added (claude.md #1/#11/#12). DETERMINATION NOT-YET (C5b storage 2025 + pre-existing C1 CC_REGULAR 2025 +2.15 TWh inherited from neiso-25). See docs/multi-iso/neiso-ps-undercycling-diagnosis-2026-06.md.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-neiso-26-ps-diag.js", "id": "2026-06-23-neiso-26-ps-diag", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 26 ps diag", "shorthand": "neiso-26-ps-diag", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-23", "definition": "real daily AGT (EIA-narrative prints + Transco shape), retires demand-convexity proxy", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-neiso-27-agt-daily.js", "id": "2026-06-23-neiso-27-agt-daily", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 27 agt daily", "shorthand": "neiso-27-agt-daily", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-24", "definition": "neiso-27 + EIA-930 hydro repin + storage vintage ramp + renewable/storage retirement ramp", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-neiso-28-hydro-codramp.js", "id": "2026-06-24-neiso-28-hydro-codramp", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 28 hydro codramp", "shorthand": "neiso-28-hydro-codramp", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-24", "definition": "neiso-28 + biomass measured must-run injection + like-for-like total comparison", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-neiso-29-biomass-mustrun.js", "id": "2026-06-24-neiso-29-biomass-mustrun", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 29 biomass mustrun", "shorthand": "neiso-29-biomass-mustrun", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-24", "definition": "(DIAGNOSTIC; keeper stays neiso-29) neiso-29 + OTHER residual class vintage-carry (2025 OTHER 2.61->2.78 TWh, measured EIA-923 like biomass): small correct fix (C1 +3.64->+3.56, C2 +3.0%->+2.5%). Solved on current main, so it also surfaces a8b0e55 (CC nameplate ~75%->92%) dropping NEISO prices 13-18% into under-prediction (C3a -15.9/-14.0/-8.1%) and storage -76% -- a SEPARATE NEISO CC-availability item, not the biomass/OTHER work.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-neiso-30-other-carry.js", "id": "2026-06-24-neiso-30-other-carry", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 30 other carry", "shorthand": "neiso-30-other-carry", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-24", "definition": "PROBE (rejected, not a keeper). Tests the bucket-A #1 scarcity hypothesis: steepen the NYISO locational RCPF reserve demand curves from the linear-ramp-to-zero stand-in to the published top-25%-span shape (critical_mw = 0.75 x requirement; FERC ER21-502 sets the NYCA 30-min $750 max at 1,965 = 0.75 x 2,620), plus the Ravenswood steam-HR correction. Finding: the steeper published curve DEEPENS the downstate tail faithfully (NYC max $138->$890, 2023 14h >$300) but does NOT add C3c tail HOURS (2024 0h, 2025 12h, unchanged) because the tail COUNT is incidence-gated by the model\u2019s too-loose downstate reserve headroom (idle NYC peakers provide ample reserve; perfect-foresight import over-service backs down downstate gas), not by the demand-curve slope. The deeper few-hour spikes push C3a mean LMP to +12% (over the +-8% band) for no C3c gain. CONCLUSION: the RCPF demand-curve SHAPE is not the C3c lever; the lever is downstate reserve INCIDENCE (locational headroom / import discipline) \u2014 the documented next structural step, out of scope as it would touch the nyiso-24 import reconciliation. The curve steepening is therefore NOT carried into the keeper (nyiso 25 steam-merit), which retains only the Ravenswood merit-order fix.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-nyiso-25-scarcity-merit.js", "id": "2026-06-24-nyiso-25-scarcity-merit", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 25 rcpf-steep (PROBE)", "shorthand": "nyiso-25-scarcity-merit", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-24", "definition": "KEEPER. Re-solves the nyiso-24 import-reconciliation keeper config (byte-identical flags) with ONE model-side data correction (data.fleet.MIXED_FACILITY_STEAM_HR): the Ravenswood (plant 2500, NYC) mixed CC+ST facility had its ~1.7 GW steam units inheriting the 8.8 MMBtu/MWh plant-blended heat rate (the efficient combined cycle\u2019s efficiency leaking into the steam row), so the big NYC steam unit cleared AHEAD of idle NYC combined cycle on merit (the 2023 CC_REGULAR -4.06 / ST_GAS +3.55 TWh inversion). Recovered the steam units\u2019 own HR (9.5) by backing the CC (~7.5) out of the 8.8 generation-weighted blend; restores the physically-correct merit order (CC ahead of steam). CC_REGULAR improves EVERY year (2023 -4.06->-3.47, 2024 -3.21->-2.61, 2025 -1.20->-0.6) and the 2023 ST_GAS over-run shrinks (+3.55->+2.76). A rule-#11 measured-data correction, forward-reproducible, not fitted to a residual. Tradeoff (kept per rule #1): C3a 2025 mean LMP +9.3% (just over +-8%) because import-constrained NYC over-relies on Ravenswood steam as marginal \u2014 a discovered symptom of the NYC import-incidence root cause (out of scope), not a reason to revert the correct HR. The RCPF scarcity tail (C3c) is unchanged: it is incidence-gated, not curve-gated (see the nyiso 25 rcpf-steep PROBE). Determination NOT-YET (the hard EIA-930/923 gas basis floor, ledgered).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-nyiso-25-steam-merit.js", "id": "2026-06-24-nyiso-25-steam-merit", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 25 steam merit", "shorthand": "nyiso-25-steam-merit", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-25", "definition": "NEISO CC_REGULAR committed min-load tranche re-anchored 0.92->1.27 (=econ_high) per measured CAMPD min-stable-load HR (1.30x avg vs full-load 0.93x); min-load priced at-or-above full-load SRMC, removes wall-masked cheap min-load block. a8b0e55 kept ON. vs neiso-30.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-neiso-31-cc-minload.js", "id": "2026-06-25-neiso-31-cc-minload", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 31 cc minload", "shorthand": "neiso-31-cc-minload", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-25", "definition": "vs neiso-31: dispatch-identical (CC min-load 1.27 re-anchor, a8b0e55 ON, biomass must-run, OTHER vintage-carry, measured AGT daily gas overlay all unchanged). Change is a BENCHMARK/attestation reconcile of NEISO solar: model & benchmark both on EIA-930 grid basis (already via actuals_source); documents that EIA-923 ISNE SUN (3.70/4.53 TWh) includes ~2.8-3.2 TWh distribution-connected/net-metered BTM PV netted into the EIA-930 net-load demand series, excluded from both sides by construction. Energy balance unchanged.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-neiso-32-btm-solar.js", "id": "2026-06-25-neiso-32-btm-solar", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 32 btm solar", "shorthand": "neiso-32-btm-solar", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-25", "definition": "vs neiso-32 (keeper): CT AS/RUC out-of-merit deployment-floor overlay REMOVED (ct_deployment_overlay OFF); every other flag/code path byte-identical. Determination-neutral AND dispatch-neutral: the overlay only ever injected 0.076/0.041/0.054 TWh/yr (<0.1% of generation), so removing it leaves gas-family 54.23/58.81/61.60 TWh, system mean LMP 33.95/38.91/66.70 $/MWh and CO2 unchanged, and the verdict stays NOT-YET with the IDENTICAL basis (undocumented fuelmix / price_tail / storage). The overlay was the calibration log's 'most answer-injecting' measured-CEMS floor yet did not flip any gate (CT_PEAKER is structurally under-produced ~0.5-0.9 TWh/yr by the energy-only LP \u2014 a reserves/local-reliability MODEL MISS the 0.04 TWh floor never closed). Removing it is strictly cleaner per CLAUDE.md #12 (one fewer measured-outcome injection) at zero cost to the backcast.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-neiso-33-no-ctfloor.js", "id": "2026-06-25-neiso-33-no-ctfloor", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 33 no ctfloor", "shorthand": "neiso-33-no-ctfloor", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-25", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle nyiso_26_cc-nameplate.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-26-cc-nameplate.js", "id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-26-cc-nameplate", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 26 cc nameplate", "shorthand": "nyiso-26-cc-nameplate", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-25", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle nyiso_27_cc-offer.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-27-cc-offer.js", "id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-27-cc-offer", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 27 cc offer", "shorthand": "nyiso-27-cc-offer", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-25", "definition": "REJECTED PROBE (keeper stays nyiso-27 cc-offer). Re-grounds _NYISO_OFFER_CURVE CC_REGULAR/CC_CHP/ST_GAS committed/econ_low/econ_high to NYISO's OWN CAMPD incremental-HR medians (derive_campd_marginal_hr.py, NY+NJ CEMS pooled 2023-25), removing the ERCOT-borrowed CC reach (1.21/1.24) and ST_GAS shape (1.10/1.45). Merit order preserved (CC econ_high eff HR 7.2/7.7 < ST committed 8.7). Result: the steam-side re-level nearly halves the 2024 ST_GAS under-run (-4.14->-2.22 TWh) and confirms steam was over-priced, but the bare CEMS marginal HR omits the competitive offer MARKUP (cost recovery + inframarginal rent) that NYISO has no offer disclosure to measure, cratering C3a mean LMP to -24.0%/-26.5%/-23.5% across 2023-25. Rule #1: a run missing real structure (the markup) is not a keeper. Carry-forward: ground a NYISO markup ON TOP of the native marginal HR (run 29).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-28-native-hr.js", "id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-28-native-hr", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 28 native-hr (PROBE)", "shorthand": "nyiso-28-native-hr", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-25", "definition": "REJECTED PROBE (keeper stays nyiso-27 cc-offer). Path A of the downstate synchronised-reserve fix: a NYISO-only default-off lever adding a NYC 10-min SPINNING sub-requirement (0.5 x NYC 10-min total = 250 MW, the published 1/2-largest-contingency rule) supplied by an ONLINE-bounded reserve class (R <= sum online P), forcing idle NYC peakers to commit. Confirms the mechanism (CT_PEAKER 2023 -1.30->-0.92, 2024 -1.51->-1.05 TWh; CC over-run down) but INSUFFICIENT for the tail (C3c >$300 unchanged 1/0/8; C3a 2024 -9.7%->-11.0%) -- the grounded 250 MW spin clears cheaply and never fires the RCPF, exactly PR #877's prediction. Scopes path B (zonal/family commitment-aware reserve). Lever reverted on the branch; implementation preserved in the bundle model_changes.diff.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-29-synch-reserve.js", "id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-29-synch-reserve", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 29 synch-reserve [probe]", "shorthand": "nyiso-29-synch-reserve", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-25", "definition": "NYISO import reconciliation made forecast-aware: backcast still targets the measured EIA-930 schedule (this run); forecast targets the neighbor's forecast net position (nyiso_forward_net_import_twh) or relaxes to the priced seam. Backcast path unchanged vs nyiso-27-cc-offer; re-solve confirms metrics hold.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-30-fwd-band.js", "id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-30-fwd-band", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 30 fwd band", "shorthand": "nyiso-30-fwd-band", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-25", "definition": "REJECTED PROBE (keeper stays nyiso-27 cc-offer). Path B of the downstate synchronised-reserve fix: commitment-gated spinning reserve (MILP-free). The NYC 250 MW spinning family rides the ordinary class-1 (quick-start) headroom while the P2 commitment screen zeroes decommitted availability, so the row equals Sum_online(pmax - P) -- the physically-correct synchronised headroom -- and a new reserve_adequacy_commit force-commits NYC quick-start to the measured 250 MW requirement. RESULT: a true no-op -- every scored metric reproduces the keeper essentially exactly (C1 CT_PEAKER 2023 -1.30/2024 -1.51 TWh; C3c >$300 1/0/8 vs actual 10/12/42; C3a 2024 -9.7%), and P1==P2 prices. ROOT CAUSE: the committed NYC quick-start fleet holds ~1,700 MW of genuine online headroom in the tightest hours vs a 500 MW 10-min / 250 MW spin requirement, so the measured reserve requirement is NON-BINDING and no RCPF tail can form -- commitment removes only offline pmax, not the dominant committed-but-backed-off headroom. Both path A (nyiso-29 online proxy) and path B are thus confirmed insufficient with grounded inputs; the >$300 downstate tail stays an accepted, ledgered open frontier on nyiso-27 (closing it would need an ungrounded requirement/adder, forbidden by rule #12). Wiring kept as a NYISO-only default-off scaffold; implementation in the bundle model_changes.diff.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-31-synch-commit.js", "id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-31-synch-commit", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 31 synch-commit [probe]", "shorthand": "nyiso-31-synch-commit", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-26", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle 160.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-26-160-as-aware-commit.js", "id": "2026-06-26-160-as-aware-commit", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "160 as aware commit", "shorthand": "160-as-aware-commit", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-26", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle 161.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-26-161-reserve-supply-cap.js", "id": "2026-06-26-161-reserve-supply-cap", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "161 reserve supply cap", "shorthand": "161-reserve-supply-cap", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-26", "definition": "NEW KEEPER (supersedes nyiso-27 cc-offer). The documented run-29 carry-forward from the rejected nyiso-28 native-hr probe: re-level the legacy gas-steam (ST_GAS) offer from the ERCOT-shaped rising ramp (committed 0.97 / econ_low 1.10 / econ_high 1.45) to NYISO's OWN measured CAMPD marginal heat rate x a grounded competitive markup (committed 1.05 / econ_low 1.08 / econ_high 1.13). Markup = the CC class's own defensible reach ratio (keeper CC econ_high 1.21 / native CC marginal 0.925 = 1.31x) applied to the flat steam native marginal HR (~0.82-0.83); CC reach UNCHANGED at the nyiso-27 keeper (the markup that holds the clearing price). Merit order preserved (steam eff HR 11.1-12.0 > CC 9.4, < CT 16.1; no inversion). Result -- HARD C1 fuel-mix IMPROVES across the board: 2023 ST_GAS -1.26 -> -0.01 TWh (now PASS, the flat measured band reproduces measured steam volume almost EXACTLY -> offer level validated a priori, not residual-fitted), CC_REGULAR +1.70 -> +0.99 (PASS); 2024 ST_GAS -4.14 -> -3.28, CC_REGULAR +2.09 -> +1.74. dispatch_corr stays PASS (gas r=0.91/0.84/0.80). SOFT C3a mean LMP regresses to a documented CAVEAT (2023 in-band -> -10.5%, 2024 -9.7% -> -11.2%): the keeper-27 steep steam ramp was a COMPENSATING over-pricing that propped up the mid-merit price; removing it (the measured-grounded move) exposes the real root cause -- the missing reserve-scarcity / RCPF tail (same cause as the CT_PEAKER C1 miss), confirmed NON-CLOSABLE with grounded inputs by nyiso-29 (online-proxy spin) and nyiso-31 (commitment-gated spin). Kept per CLAUDE.md rule #1 (a measured mechanism stays in even though the fit worsens; reverting would reach C3a through a mechanism that isn't real and re-open the volume miss; raising steam to chase C3a would distort the volume-validated level, rule #12) -- the same promotion principle as nyiso-25. Import/export: net-interchange import reconciliation + priced node unchanged; the downstate interface-TTC congestion separation (U7) stays data-blocked. Governance C6 PASS; determination NOT-YET (price_shape/price_tail = the genuine reserve-scarcity model gap, unledgered MODEL MISS, same open frontier on every NYISO keeper). Reproduce: the nyiso-27 keeper flags + --offer-curve-json '{\"ST_GAS\": {\"committed\": 1.05, \"econ_low\": 1.08, \"econ_high\": 1.13}}'.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-26-nyiso-32-steam-markup.js", "id": "2026-06-26-nyiso-32-steam-markup", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 32 steam-markup [keeper]", "shorthand": "nyiso-32-steam-markup", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-27", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle 162fwdas.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-27-162fwdas-full-fwdas-coopt.js", "id": "2026-06-27-162fwdas-full-fwdas-coopt", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "162fwdas full fwdas coopt", "shorthand": "162fwdas-full-fwdas-coopt", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-27", "definition": "Forward AS requirement (G3): run159 multi-product co-opt with each AS product (RegUp/RRS/ECRS/NonSpin) set from a forward formula of forecast drivers (net-load, ramp, VRE-share, forecast-error, load-ratio) instead of the measured AS Plan. Modeled-vs-measured requirement MW holds (total up-AS 0.94/0.98/1.02x). Acute/tail: 2025 clean (MAE 2.5), 2023 acute held; 2024 May acute under-fires ($21.7 vs measured-req $40) \u2014 ERCOT's discretionary day-specific acute AS uplift has no forward-driver analogue (not pinned; honesty gate). Measured DAM-AS overlay (run157) stays the pre-RTC+B bridge for the discretionary component.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-27-163-forward-as-requirement.js", "id": "2026-06-27-163-forward-as-requirement", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "163 forward as requirement", "shorthand": "163-forward-as-requirement", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-27", "definition": "Endogenous storage energy-vs-AS co-opt (G5): run163 + ercot_storage_as_endogenous + RTOLCAP reserve-supply cap. The battery CHOOSES energy vs upward-AS inside the multi-product co-opt (full power cap, room competes with arbitrage on the same cap, priced by the per-product AS demand curves) \u2014 REPLACING the measured 60-Day DAM battery AS-award reservation. Cleared storage AS counts toward the measured RTOLCAP online supply (which already includes batteries); the cap is a pure supply bound (the additive ORDC adder is gated off \u2014 the co-opt prices endogenously), so acute/tail HOLDS vs run163: acute 8/24/26 $24.6/$21.7/$30.2, 2025 clean. Measured-vs-modeled split (mean MW, storage-first UPPER bound): modeled AS 2427/4036/5680 vs measured 1249/2045/2824 (~2x), AS share 95/93/89% \u2014 AS-dominated and rising with the fleet as in reality, but over-holds AS vs the measured level (documented residual, NOT retuned to it; the split is the LP's choice, validated against the award not pinned to it). 2023/24 annual under-price inherited from run163's broad-May energy-base residual, not this lever.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-27-164-storage-as-endogenous.js", "id": "2026-06-27-164-storage-as-endogenous", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "164 storage as endogenous", "shorthand": "164-storage-as-endogenous", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-27", "definition": "(PROBE) Priced-interchange + dual-limb weather floor: registers import/export as its own dashboard class (the fuelRows 'interchange' row, model vs EIA-930 net interchange) the same way CAISO/PJM measure it. Surfaces a real miss \u2014 the NEISO priced import/export node OVER-IMPORTS (net import model vs actual 18.1/15.1 (2023), 18.8/10.3 (2024), 27.4/8.1 TWh (2025)): the import tranches are sized to the import envelope, not calibrated to net energy, so the LP floods cheap imports and under-runs domestic gas. Keeper stays the measured-schedule run (neiso 35); priced mode needs import-tranche price/capacity calibration before it is a keeper. Carries the new temperature-correlated reliability floor (CT_PEAKER hot-limb TMAX, COAL/ST_GAS cold-limb TMIN).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-27-neiso-34-import-export.js", "id": "2026-06-27-neiso-34-import-export", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 34 import export", "shorthand": "neiso-34-import-export", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-27", "definition": "neiso-37 = the neiso-33 keeper recipe (measured Algonquin/EIA-MA-citygate DAILY gas-hub basis overlay + dual-fuel oil re-attribution + EIA-930-pinned hydro budget with 2024 plant-capacity backfill + biomass/OTHER measured must-run + historic outage overlay + storage vintage ramp) WITH the dual-limb temperature-correlated reliability floor (neiso_temp_reliability_floor: CT_PEAKER hot-limb on TMAX, COAL/ST_GAS cold-limb on TMIN) added on top. Restores the measured daily gas-hub basis overlay (--gas-hub-basis-daily) that neiso-36 had accidentally dropped: with only the flat MONTHLY hub, the within-month cold-day Algonquin spikes never crossed distillate parity, so the physical dual-fuel gas->oil switch never tripped, modeled oil collapsed ~1.6->0.05 TWh and relabeled back onto gas-CC (gas-family C2 +4.3%, FAIL). With the daily overlay restored the switch trips on the real cold-day prints, 2025 oil returns to 1.60 TWh and the gas family lands 60.85 vs EIA-930 59.76 (+1.8%, C2 PASS). Verdict CALIBRATED-WITH-CAVEATS: 1 HARD C1 CC_REGULAR 2023/24 gas-vs-EIA-930-OTHER fold-in categorization caveat + carried soft C3c price-tail and C5b storage caveats; C6 attested. Promotes NEISO keeper 33 -> 37.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-27-neiso-37-weather-floor.js", "id": "2026-06-27-neiso-37-weather-floor", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 37 weather floor", "shorthand": "neiso-37-weather-floor", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-27", "definition": "NEW KEEPER (supersedes nyiso-32 steam-markup). Adds a temperature-keyed DOWNSTATE CT_PEAKER local-reliability floor (--nyiso-ct-reliability-floor) on top of the nyiso-32 steam-markup keeper -- the CAISO local-RA CT floor ported to NYISO's cable-constrained downstate load pockets (NYC zone J, Long Island zone K, Lower Hudson). On hot afternoons the downstate cooling load climbs and the UPNY-SENY / LI-cable import limits bind, so fast-start GTs are held online for local capacity-area reliability; an energy-only LP imports cheap upstate/NYC CC instead and under-runs CT_PEAKER. Floor = clip(base + slope*(TMAX-25), base, cap) x available downstate-CT capacity over HB14-21, with slope=0.053/degC, cap=0.68, base=0.13 -- ALL regressed a priori from the measured downstate CAMPD CT_PEAKER evening CF vs NYC daily max temperature (NOAA GHCN), pooled 2023-2025 (scripts/derive_nyiso_ct_reliability_floor.py; archived data/raw/nyiso-weather/). A physical heat->commitment rule, forward-reproducible (a forecast year pins a weather year, hence a TMAX series), NOT a CEMS pin and NOT residual-tuned (CT_PEAKER lands UNDER actual, so not over-forced). Result -- the HARD C1 fuel-mix improves decisively: CT_PEAKER (the open frontier on EVERY prior NYISO keeper, and confirmed non-closable via a grounded reserve REQUIREMENT by nyiso-29/31) 2023 -1.73 -> -0.85 TWh and 2024 -1.92 -> -0.85 TWh, FAIL -> PASS both scored years; the complementary CC_REGULAR over-run drops 2023 +0.99 -> +0.63 (PASS) and 2024 +1.74 -> +1.25 (FAIL -> PASS). 2023 now has every gas class in C1 tolerance. dispatch_corr stays PASS (gas r=0.91/0.85/0.80). TRADEOFF (kept per rule #1): C3a mean LMP regresses ~1.3pp (2023 -10.5 -> -11.7%, 2024 -11.2 -> -12.7%, 2025 -7.3 -> -8.6%) because the floor is a min-gen (must-run) representation -- it recovers the local-RA ENERGY but adds inframarginal supply rather than letting the peakers SET a scarcity price; the price side (C3a/C3c) remains the ledgered reserve-scarcity frontier (an ORDC/reserve-scarcity demand curve, non-closable with grounded inputs per nyiso-29/31). Remaining C1 miss: 2024 ST_GAS -3.77 TWh (the floor pulls some evening energy from downstate steam too). Most structurally faithful NYISO config to date: it adds a real, measured, forward-reproducible local-reliability mechanism that closes the largest C1 miss -- the rule-#1 'right market structure' step the reserve-requirement attempts could not take. Governance C6 PASS; determination NOT-YET (2024 ST_GAS C1 + price_shape/price_tail reserve-scarcity gap). Reproduce: nyiso-32 keeper flags + --nyiso-ct-reliability-floor (now the live default for NYISO).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-27-nyiso-33-ct-tempfloor.js", "id": "2026-06-27-nyiso-33-ct-tempfloor", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 33 ct-tempfloor [keeper]", "shorthand": "nyiso-33-ct-tempfloor", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-27", "definition": "nyiso-34 builds on the nyiso-33 ct-tempfloor keeper and adds a temperature-keyed downstate ST_GAS (gas-steam) local-reliability floor (--nyiso-st-reliability-floor), the ST-fleet companion to the CT floor. MECHANISM: NYISO's downstate steam fleet (NYC zone J Ravenswood/Arthur Kill/Astoria; Long Island; Capital) runs a persistent in-city/cable-islanded reliability baseline an energy-only LP zeroes out (it imports cheaper CC instead), so the backcast under-runs ST_GAS. The floor is a persistent 24h baseline with an evening cooling hot-limb layered over HB14-21 via maximum, applied per-unit pro-rata (each in-pocket unit floored at frac x its OWN available capacity), keyed per zone to its load-center daily max temperature (Islip/Central Park/Albany, NOAA GHCN). Carries two structural corrections it depends on (when-available CF basis; the second per the attestation) plus a benchmark-basis fix, all flagged as methodology fixes, not residual tunes. Verdict CALIBRATED-WITH-CAVEATS: 4 documented caveats + storage unscored; C6 attested. [SUPERSEDED 2026-07-02: this verdict reflects the run's own era (sha 180d579 + dirty floor patch, legacy injectors since deleted); the bundle re-scores NOT-YET on current main and the run is not reproducible there. NYISO keeper is now 2026-07-02-nyiso-39-priced-interchange, which reproduces the same determination on the unified engine.]", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-27-nyiso-34-st-tempfloor.js", "id": "2026-06-27-nyiso-34-st-tempfloor", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 34 st tempfloor", "shorthand": "nyiso-34-st-tempfloor", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-27", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle 162e.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-27-run162e-merit-order-energy.js", "id": "2026-06-27-run162e-merit-order-energy", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "run162e merit order energy", "shorthand": "run162e-merit-order-energy", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-27", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle 162f.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-27-run162f-merit-order-keeper.js", "id": "2026-06-27-run162f-merit-order-keeper", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "run162f merit order keeper", "shorthand": "run162f-merit-order-keeper", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-27", "definition": "Structural forward build (G4): run164 + the enrollment-driven forward load-resource RRS-UFR credit (ercot_load_resource_reserve) \u2014 the LAST measured AS *input* on the supply side given a forward analogue. Load Resources provide Responsive Reserve via under-frequency relays (RRS-UFR, an ERCOT-protocol load-side-only service the co-opt omitted); the credit nets it off the RRS product's reserve-balance RHS after the demand curve is sized to the gross peak (tail preserved). Mode-aware: BACKCAST reads the measured NP3-911 (~0.9 GW, byte-identical), FORECAST reads the enrollment forward lr_rrs(t)=enrolled_DR_MW(year)x mean-1 availability shape (enrollment ~0.9 GW today -> ~1.4 GW protocol cap). Composes with the RTOLCAP cap (300b89c) and endogenous storage AS (ea8c656) with no double count (load resources are outside RTOLCAP). Acute HELD vs run164: May 8/24/26 $24.6/$21.7/$30.2; tail RELAXES per the designed forward response (more load-side reserve -> fewer scarcity hours): h>$200 36/4/1 vs run164 57/12/3, model annual 30.0/20.7/33.0 vs 34.3/21.3/33.1. Measured-vs-forward load-resource RRS (mean MW): measured 884/904/787 vs enrollment 780/840/900 (0.88-1.14x \u2014 trend reproduces the present level, not pinned to the year wiggle). DETERMINATION NOT-YET (same inherited broad-May energy-base / merit-order residual as run164: fuelmix/sysvol/price \u2014 separate track, NOT retuned here). Honesty gate: enrollment from a forward trend, never the cleared MW pinned to a price. ERCOT broad-month keeper remains run157.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-27-run165-load-resource-rrs.js", "id": "2026-06-27-run165-load-resource-rrs", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "run165 load resource rrs", "shorthand": "run165-load-resource-rrs", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-28", "definition": "caiso 35 reference-price seam: replaces the measured per-hub OASIS hub-LMP ladder (caiso_per_hub_intertie) with the forward-native reference-price seam (caiso_reference_price_seam) \u2014 both legs of each WECC corridor priced from (HH + gas_basis) x HR x load-shape +/- hurdle, CARB border carbon on import leg, export at hub - hurdle; HR anchors from measured Malin/Palo-Verde annual-mean RT LMP / delivered gas (derive_caiso_seam_hr_by_year.py; rule #11/#12); corridor ATC switched to the forward one-sided import envelope (caiso_corridor_atc_forward) so the export direction is free to the physical TTC. All other caiso 34 levers verbatim. Net-interchange error narrows to +3.56/+2.78/+4.40 TWh (2023/24/25, was chronically negative in prior keepers) and diurnal shape r improves to 0.88/0.93/0.89, confirming the seam correctly liberates midday solar export. However the CAISO price body-overprice is unchanged from caiso 34: CA-zone avg LMP ~100/63/69 vs actual ~44/33/34 (C3a +128%/+90%/+104%; C3b NRMSE 0.77/0.98/1.09; C3c scarcity hours 744/0/0 vs actual 21/35/8), and C6 governance gate is unattested (no calibration_attestation.json). Determination NOT-YET: failing C3a/C3b/C3c price-body gates and unattested C6.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-28-caiso-35-reference-seam.js", "id": "2026-06-28-caiso-35-reference-seam", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 35 reference seam", "shorthand": "caiso-35-reference-seam", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-28", "definition": "(PROBE - REJECTED, not a keeper; caiso 35 remains the keeper) Tested the hypothesis that caiso 35's CA-zone LMP over-price is the reference-seam price being too smooth midday: deepened the WECC seam net-load convexity (DSW exp 1.0->2.5; PNW gross->net exp 1.5), lowered the midday floor to a small negative, dropped the inflated partial-coverage 2023 hr_by_year. RESULT: the body did NOT improve (CA-zone LMP 98.7/69.7/75.3 vs actual 43.8/33.0/33.6; 744 h >$200 in 2023 unchanged) and interchange REGRESSED (2023 err +3.56->+8.49 TWh; 2024 +2.78->-4.95 flip to under-import; 2024 diurnal r 0.93->0.49). DIAGNOSIS: the body over-price is DOMESTIC, not the seam - midday the marginal unit is economic gas_cc bidding ~89 (implied HR ~25 = fuel + P1 startup-amort + reserve co-opt), with NO model midday solar surplus (solar fully used, CA still needs 6.5 GW gas_cc), and the model already imports ~4x the measured midday volume. Source reverted to caiso 35. See results/calibration/DIAGNOSIS-caiso36-body-overprice-domestic-2026-06-28.md.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-28-caiso-36-midday-convexity.js", "id": "2026-06-28-caiso-36-midday-convexity", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 36 midday convexity", "shorthand": "caiso-36-midday-convexity", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-28", "definition": "caiso 37 offer-curve: give CAISO its own ISO-specific gas offer curve (_CAISO_OFFER_CURVE) instead of the silently-inherited ERCOT heat-rate multipliers + ERCOT CT 13.15x $5,000-ORDC wall. The fix for the DOMESTIC CA-zone midday/body LMP over-price diagnosed in caiso 36 (the seam is fine; gas_cc offer level sets the midday body). CC_REGULAR re-grounded to the measured CAMPD CC marginal-HR shape (econ 1.06/1.27 -> 0.95/1.21, physical duct-fire peak 2.25 kept); CT_PEAKER peak capped 13.15 -> 4.0 (CAISO $1,000-2,000 soft cap, PJM's reasoning), econ re-grounded to the DMM DEB cost-plus- adder shape (1.27/1.98 -> 1.10/1.50), committed start hurdle 1.55 -> 1.35 (NYISO-grounded, CTs serve the evening ramp). Grounded in CAISO DMM market structure + measured CAMPD heat rates, NOT the price residual (rules #1/#11). CC_CHP/CT_CHP/ST_GAS/coal keep the generic defaults; ERCOT/PJM/other ISOs byte-identical. All caiso 35 seam levers verbatim.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-28-caiso-37-offer-curve.js", "id": "2026-06-28-caiso-37-offer-curve", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 37 offer curve", "shorthand": "caiso-37-offer-curve", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-28", "definition": "neiso-39 = the neiso-38 keeper recipe EXACTLY (measured Algonquin/EIA-MA-citygate DAILY gas-hub basis overlay + dual-fuel oil re-attribution + EIA-930-pinned hydro budget with 2024 plant-capacity backfill + biomass/OTHER measured must-run + historic CAMPD outage overlay + storage vintage ramp + the dual-limb temperature-correlated reliability floor + the neiso_floor_outage_exempt Merrimack/ST_GAS availability fix) WITH one structural-parity refactor and NO behaviour change: NEISO's gas offer curve is consolidated into a single grounded, commented _NEISO_OFFER_CURVE dict deep-merged on iso==NEISO, at parity with _PJM_OFFER_CURVE / _NYISO_OFFER_CURVE. Previously NEISO carried one scattered inline override (CC_REGULAR committed 1.27) and silently inherited the generic ERCOT-derived 'else' for every other gas band (incl. the 13.15x CT_PEAKER peak wall). Step 1 (this run) is behavior-preserving: every band is transcribed exactly from NEISO's prior effective curve, so config builds for all 7 ISOs are byte-identical AND this 3-year solve reproduces the neiso-38 keeper byte-for-byte on every metric (gas 54.19/58.72/61.42 TWh, oil 0.35/0.25/1.59, hydro 8.70/7.33/5.11, LMP 33.93/38.88/66.60). Step 2 (grounded re-level: shape from NEISO's own CAMPD HRs, cap the ERCOT-derived CT peak to ~3-4x vs the ISO-NE IMM/Potomac EMM) is deferred and documented as a follow-up. At promotion (2026-06-28) this run's determination read as calibrated-with-caveats (C2/C3a/C3b/C4/C5a/C6 PASS; C1 CC_REGULAR gas-vs-EIA-930-OTHER fold-in + C3c price-tail + C5b storage caveats carried unchanged from neiso-38). Current determination is NOT-YET: a subsequent NEISO bench refresh (accompanying the neiso-41 relfloor-rebuild run) moved the 2025 gas-family target, and neiso-39's unchanged dispatch now fails C2 system-volume (2025 gas +2.8%), which is undocumented in this bundle's exceptions ledger and so is not carried as an accepted caveat -- a benchmark-side drift, not a dispatch regression in neiso-39 itself. Promotes NEISO keeper 37 -> 39 (and lands the neiso-38 availability fix, previously only a patch file); note a newer NEISO run (neiso-41-relfloor-rebuild) already exists in the registry.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-28-neiso-39-offer-curve.js", "id": "2026-06-28-neiso-39-offer-curve", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 39 offer curve", "shorthand": "neiso-39-offer-curve", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-28", "definition": "pjm 61 consolidate: keep the structurally-correct pjm 59 fixes (CT_PEAKER dispatch fix committed 0.878->1.25 / econ_low 0.879->1.05 so CTs clear as ~3% CF peakers not ~100% baseload; ct_intermediate_split threshold=30%; CC_REGULAR peak 3.4->5.0; cc_duct_peaking_cap_pct 12->18% uniform ceiling) + add offer_curve_smoothing_mid=0.35 (convex econ ramp). DROP the harmful pjm 60 ERCOT ports (battery_dispatch_adder=$10, storage_daily_cycling, per-plant cc_duct_peaking flag, chp_steam_following). Price-tail decompression is a separate structural build (energy+reserve co-opt) blocked on memory (co-opt OOMs >16 GB) and commitment posture \u2014 see docs/multi-iso/pjm-reserve-ordc.md.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-28-pjm-61-consolidate.js", "id": "2026-06-28-pjm-61-consolidate", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 61 consolidate", "shorthand": "pjm-61-consolidate", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-28", "definition": "(PROBE, rejected) pjm 62 coopt deliverable: pjm 61 consolidate config + energy_reserve_coopt with deliverable reserve-supply scoping (pjm_reserve_supply_cap = \u03a3 ramp10[eligible] + pjm_reserve_online_gated \u03c1=1.0). Measured PJM Primary req (~3.4 GW) vs published two-step ORDC, cleared inside the LP. RESULT: reserve cleared $0 in every hour, so prices are IDENTICAL to the pjm 61 energy-only keeper-candidate (2023 26.42/56.50, 2024 24.89/58.66, 2025 35.06/76.97 avg/max). This is the expected honest confirmation (handoff step 2): the deliverable cap is ~50 GW and online \u03c1\u00b7\u03a3P is huge \u2014 both \u226b the ~3.4 GW requirement on a ~180 GW fleet \u2014 because the perfect-foresight LP is not tight when PJM's real RT was tight. The top-tail decompression needs per-gen R[g]\u2264ramp10[g] (reserve competing with energy on the marginal unit) PLUS commitment tightening, NOT zone-aggregate scoping. Memtest correction: the zone-aggregate co-opt FITS the 15 GB box (~14.5 GB peak/yr), so the doc's prior 'cannot run any co-opt on 15 GB' claim was stale. Nothing fitted to the residual (claude.md #11).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-28-pjm-62-coopt-deliverable.js", "id": "2026-06-28-pjm-62-coopt-deliverable", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 62 coopt deliverable", "shorthand": "pjm-62-coopt-deliverable", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-28", "definition": "(PROBE, not promoted) pjm 63 offercurve: pjm 61 consolidate energy-only config + a one-lever offer-curve reshape. CC_REGULAR econ_high 1.38->1.60 + peak 5.0->6.0 (steepen CC upper econ ramp + widen duct-firing scarcity band); CT_PEAKER committed 1.25->1.10 / econ_low 1.05->0.95 / econ_high 1.40->1.20 / peak 4.0->3.0 (lower the whole peaker curve). No reserve co-opt. RESULT: lifted the price-duration BODY ~+0.5 $/MWh avg/yr (2023 26.42->26.87, 2024 24.89->25.38, 2025 35.06->35.59 avg) but the TOP TAIL stayed flat/down (max 2023 56.50->56.46, 2024 58.66->57.57, 2025 76.97->76.83). Diagnostic: the CC duct-firing peak tranche rarely clears so widening it didn't lift the max; lowering CT_PEAKER capped the max (the CT peaker is the model's top-hour marginal/ceiling unit). Offer-curve tuning (CLAUDE.md #1 second step), nothing fitted to the residual (#11). Memory peak 11.8 GB.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-28-pjm-63-offercurve.js", "id": "2026-06-28-pjm-63-offercurve", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 63 offercurve", "shorthand": "pjm-63-offercurve", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-29", "definition": "caiso-38 replaces CAISO's EIA-923 gas price series (7-plant NorCal/SDG&E-skewed sample, $4.45/MMBtu 2024) with the measured SoCal/PG&E Citygate hub basis overlay (gas_hub_basis_overlay: EIA N3050CA3 - Henry Hub from gas_basis_by_iso_month.csv, the same mechanism as NEISO doc-08), reconciled up to the full-census CA electric-power delivered price (EIA N3045CA3 $3.84) via the measured citygate-to-burner-tip intrastate-transport adder (CAISO_CITYGATE_TRANSPORT_ADDER +0.46 = N3045CA3 - N3050CA3 2024), so the marginal CC bids its true delivered fuel cost rather than the skewed 7-plant estimate \u2014 measured, forward-reproducible, not residual-tuned (rule #11). Headline: CA-zone (NP15/ZP26/SP15) demand-weighted LMP fell 95.7/61.0/64.8 \u2192 85.7/57.1/62.5 $/MWh (2023/24/25; actual 43.8/33.0/33.6), but interchange regressed +1.33/+0.12/+2.78 \u2192 \u22122.38/\u22125.46/\u2014 TWh because the corrected lower gas exposed an import-ATC miscalibration that the inflated gas price had been masking \u2014 a discovered structural bug (rule #11), not a reason to revert. All caiso-37 offer-curve + seam levers carried verbatim; ERCOT/PJM/NYISO/NEISO byte-identical. NOT-YET: C1 (CC_REGULAR over-run, CT_PEAKER under-run 2023/24), C2 (2025 gas), C3a/b/c (LMP body/shape/tail), C4 (dispatch correlation), C5a (CO2) all remain open MODEL MISS; C6 governance unattested.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-29-caiso-38-delivered-gas.js", "id": "2026-06-29-caiso-38-delivered-gas", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 38 delivered gas", "shorthand": "caiso-38-delivered-gas", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-29", "definition": "caiso 39 import-ATC re-grounding: re-ground the WECC corridor forward-ATC base fractions (CAISO_PER_HUB_NEIGHBORS) to the measured p95 overnight net-import deliverability (PNW 0.30->0.43, DSW 0.50->0.56), closing the import-ATC compression the caiso 38 finding exposed (model import swing 2,321 vs actual 4,596 MW). Midday solar floor stays 0.30 (the p95 midday ratio overstates the typical ceiling and inverts the diurnal). Measured-deliverability grounded (rule #11/#12), NOT residual-tuned. All caiso 38 levers verbatim; ERCOT/PJM/NYISO/NEISO byte-identical.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-29-caiso-39-import-atc.js", "id": "2026-06-29-caiso-39-import-atc", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 39 import atc", "shorthand": "caiso-39-import-atc", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-29", "definition": "caiso 40: RA must-offer bridge min-load 0.40->0.26 (CAMPD-measured CA CC min stable load, CLAUDE.md #11)", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-29-caiso-40-ra-minload.js", "id": "2026-06-29-caiso-40-ra-minload", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 40 ra minload", "shorthand": "caiso-40-ra-minload", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-29", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle caiso-41-endogenous-spill.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-29-caiso-41-endogenous-spill.js", "id": "2026-06-29-caiso-41-endogenous-spill", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 41 endogenous spill", "shorthand": "caiso-41-endogenous-spill", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-29", "definition": "(PROBE) miso25 + energy_reserve_coopt=True (MISO RBDC, BPM-002/Schedule 28-A). Reserve co-opt is structurally correct but worsened the import overshot: net imports rose to 51.4/38.6/23.6 TWh (2023/24/25) vs actual 37.9/23.1/19.0 \u2014 worse than miso25's 46.7/32.4/21.4 \u2014 because reserved gas headroom is backfilled by cheap seam imports, leaving gas under-dispatched (215.7/226.0/191.4 TWh vs EIA-923 ~249/258/208). Model import flow is also too deep overnight (p50 -6869 vs actual -4330 MW in 2023) and too volatile (diurnal swing 4832 vs 1020 MW). Root cause is the seam reference price lacking an hourly shape, not reserves. Rejected; energy_reserve_coopt stays OFF for MISO until the seam price is fixed.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-29-miso-26-reserve-coopt.js", "id": "2026-06-29-miso-26-reserve-coopt", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso 26 reserve coopt", "shorthand": "miso-26-reserve-coopt", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-29", "definition": "Offer-SHAPE faithfulness fix on top of miso22 (all miso22/miso21 levers retained): correct the CC_REGULAR committed (min-stable-load) band 0.92 -> 1.20 to the measured CAMPD part-load premium (a CC's min-load $/MWh is ~30-40% above full-load SRMC), so the min-load tranche prices ABOVE the full-load body (committed > econ_high > econ_low) instead of below it; capped just under the ST_GAS non-inversion ceiling (1.227). METRIC-NEUTRAL vs miso22 (gmModel CC_REGULAR -1.5/-0.8/-0.9 TWh, the right direction on the over-run; ST_GAS / CT_PEAKER / coal / prices all <0.5 TWh / <$0.1/MWh): the committed band is a must-run min-load PRICE block whose volume is set by commitment, so it corrects merit ORDER without moving volume. Determination NOT-YET (unchanged C1/C2 misses): the CC-over/CT-under substitution is NOT offer-curve-addressable (CC excess is in its measured-flat econ body; CT deficit is gated by the missing scarcity mechanism + the 2024/25 import under-run -- separate root causes). Keeper on faithfulness grounds (correct min-load offer ordering), not on a fit gain.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-29-miso23-cc-minload-premium.js", "id": "2026-06-29-miso23-cc-minload-premium", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso23 cc-minload-premium", "shorthand": "miso23-cc-minload-premium", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-29", "definition": "Replace the PJM import seam's synthetic gas x HR reference price with measured PJM-west border-hub hourly DA LMP (equal-weight mean of CHICAGO GEN / AEP GEN / ATSI GEN hubs, PJM Data Miner). Mirrors the proven CAISO intertie-hub pattern: measured price overwrites ONLY the PJM seam import/export rows after inject_reference_price_mc; SPP/South seams keep gas x HR. All 8 import tranches get the same measured price + hurdle. NO firm floor, NO adder. Fixes the 2025 import under-run (gmModel import 21.4 vs 19.0 actual, was 5.8 keeper); 2024 moves right direction but overshoots (32.4 vs 23.1 actual, was 17.0 keeper); 2023 over-imports (46.7 vs 37.9 actual, was 39.4 keeper). The 2023/2024 overshot is price-correct: measured PJM LMP ($28-29/MWh mean) is cheaper than internal MISO clearing prices, so the LP imports the full capacity; the root cause is the CC over-run inflating domestic supply cost. CC_REGULAR down -3.4/-5.1/-4.3 TWh (right direction); CT_PEAKER unchanged (gated by missing scarcity mechanism). Determination NOT-YET (C1 CC/CT substitution unchanged). Structurally more faithful than keeper (measured price > synthetic estimate, rule #11).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-29-miso25-pjm-lmp-import.js", "id": "2026-06-29-miso25-pjm-lmp-import", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso25 pjm lmp import", "shorthand": "miso25-pjm-lmp-import", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-29", "definition": "(PROBE \u2014 REJECTED) miso25 flag set + --interchange-shaping-export-only: shape ONLY the export leg of the priced interchange node to the measured EIA-930 (month x hour-of-day) envelope, leaving imports uncapped (clear on price under the existing seam flow cap). Intent: impose the measured diurnal envelope on exports without over-starving baseload imports. RESULT: WRONG LEVER \u2014 net imports got WORSE in all 3 years (model 52.97/39.44/29.66 TWh vs miso25 46.7/32.4/21.4, actual 37.9/23.1/19.0), because shaping the export leg down to the small measured MISO export profile removed the model's balancing exports and pushed NET imports up. Diurnal swing barely moved (peak->trough 4803 MW 2023 vs miso25 4832, actual 1020): the over-deep overnight problem lives on the IMPORT leg, which export-only leaves uncapped, so it cannot fix gap (1). import-hours ~100% all years (vs 98/91/79 actual). Superseded by miso27b (full --interchange-shaping, both legs). energy_reserve_coopt and firm-import-floor stay OFF.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-29-miso27-interchange-shape-export.js", "id": "2026-06-29-miso27-interchange-shape-export", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso27 interchange shape export", "shorthand": "miso27-interchange-shape-export", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-29", "definition": "(PROBE) miso25 flag set + full --interchange-shaping (both legs, default percentile=90): shape the priced interchange node by the measured EIA-930 (month x hour-of-day) net-interchange envelope \u2014 import availability capped overnight-deep/midday-shallow, export min_gen released to the measured midday export. FIXES gap (1), the diurnal volatility: peak->trough swing drops to 1959/1636/1674 MW (2023/24/25) from miso25's ~4832, vs actual 1020/999/1471 \u2014 the measured deliverability envelope (admissible rule #12, same class as miso_seam_flow_limit) is the right structure and stays. BUT overcorrects the LEVEL: net imports now UNDERSHOOT actual \u2014 23.93/13.79/10.28 TWh vs actual 37.9/23.1/19.0 (miso25 overshot 46.7/32.4/21.4). Root cause is structural and documented in inject_interchange_shape: the full shape caps *gross* import availability at the *net*-import envelope (net << gross since MISO imports AND exports across different seams), starving baseload imports \u2014 the LP imports ~100% of hours (2023/24) but the per-hour cap binds low. Import depth too shallow at every percentile (2023 p10/p50/p90 -4502/-2714/-538 vs actual -7130/-4330/-1596). Collateral: 2025 coal over-runs (+26 TWh vs EIA-923) backfilling the missing imports; energy-balance drift -4.5/-4.8 TWh in 2024/25. Lever for the level is the code's designed INTERCHANGE_SHAPE_IMPORT_PCT 'bidir sweep' (fatten the deliverable overnight tail) -> miso27c (import_pct=97). NO firm floor, energy_reserve_coopt OFF.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-29-miso27b-interchange-shape-full.js", "id": "2026-06-29-miso27b-interchange-shape-full", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso27b interchange shape full", "shorthand": "miso27b-interchange-shape-full", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-29", "definition": "(PROBE \u2014 REJECTED) neiso-40 = neiso-39 keeper recipe + neiso_oil_burn_budget flag ON: adds an inventory-limited monthly oil-burn LP constraint (one row per oil-primary generator \u00d7 month, upper bound = EIA-923 Schedule 5 petroleum receipts converted to MWh). Goal: produce >$300 scarcity-tail hours (C3c) by endogenously pricing marginal oil MWh when the monthly budget binds in cold-snap months. NULL FINDING: F923 petroleum receipt coverage is too sparse to support the constraint \u2014 only 1-2 NEISO plants report petroleum receipts (out of 140 oil-primary generators / 5345 MW), and in 2025 only 2/12 months have any deliveries. A sparsity guard (requires \u22656 months with deliveries AND \u22655% of fleet reporting) correctly skips the constraint in all 3 years, producing a solve identical to neiso-39. The fundamental issue: F923 receipts measure fuel deliveries to tank, not inventory available to burn \u2014 zero receipts in a month doesn't mean zero oil available (plants burn from stored inventory). Dual-fuel gas units (6831 MW) are excluded from the constraint because their oil consumption isn't captured in F923 petroleum receipts (they report as gas plants). The C3c scarcity tail requires a different mechanism \u2014 likely a forward-derivable winter fuel-inventory or delivery-logistics constraint, not monthly F923 receipts.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-29-neiso-40-oil-budget.js", "id": "2026-06-29-neiso-40-oil-budget", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 40 oil budget", "shorthand": "neiso-40-oil-budget", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-29", "definition": "(PROBE \u2014 not promoted; keeper stays pjm 58 seam-border) pjm 64 offercurve: pjm 63 config + ONE lever, CT_PEAKER peak 3.0->5.0 (above pjm 61's 4.0). Keeps the pjm 63 CC_REGULAR body gain (econ_high 1.38->1.60, peak 5.0->6.0) and the lower CT_PEAKER committed/econ_low/econ_high (1.10/0.95/1.20), but raises the peaker duct-firing scarcity ceiling so it clears the lifted CC upper econ ramp \u2014 letting the marginal CC set the top-tail clearing price instead of being capped by a low peaker ceiling. RESULT: lifted ONLY the 2024 max (57.57->63.03, +5.46); 2023 (56.46) and 2025 (76.83) max and ALL years' body (avg 26.87/25.38/35.59, p50/p90) unchanged to the cent \u2014 confirms the peak tranche is the binding ceiling only in 2024 and is a rarely-reached scarcity band that never moves the body. Model max ~$56-77 vs PJM actual hundreds: the top-tail miss is structurally NOT offer-curve-reachable on this energy-only LP (matches the reserve-track conclusion). Offer-curve tuning (CLAUDE.md #1 second step), not a residual adder. Energy-only, no co-opt (pjm 62 cleared reserve $0), no P2.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-29-pjm-64-offercurve.js", "id": "2026-06-29-pjm-64-offercurve", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 64 offercurve", "shorthand": "pjm-64-offercurve", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "ERCOT reliability-floor rebuild: keeper config (run157) + per-(zone,class) full-day day-gate reliability floor", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-20-relfloor-rebuild.js", "id": "2026-06-30-20-relfloor-rebuild", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "20 relfloor rebuild", "shorthand": "20-relfloor-rebuild", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "ercot 21 cc-coal-rebalance: run157 base + reliability_floor, CC_REGULAR econ_high delta 0.0, peak delta +0.18, PRB sigmoid floor 0.74", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-21-cc-coal-rebalance.js", "id": "2026-06-30-21-cc-coal-rebalance", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "21 cc coal rebalance", "shorthand": "21-cc-coal-rebalance", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "From the run157-dam-as-overlay keeper, adds reliability_floor and moderates three offer-curve deltas that caused a 2023 LMP collapse in ercot 21 ($28.57 model vs $48.36 actual at $2.54/MWh gas): CC_REGULAR econ_high delta set to -0.10 (halfway between keeper -0.20 and ercot 21 0.0), peak delta +0.25 (between keeper +0.32 and ercot 21 +0.18), and PRB sigmoid floor 0.76 (between keeper 0.78 and ercot 21 0.74). These are offer-curve shape parameters \u2014 no adder or haircut fitted to the price residual, all levers have forward analogues. Determination: NOT-YET (C1 ST_GAS 2024 fuel-mix, C2 gas 2025 system volume, and C3b price shape 2023\u20132024 remain out of band; C3a 2024 mean LMP +8.4% accepted as measured-input limitation per the attestation).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-22-cc-coal-rebalance.js", "id": "2026-06-30-22-cc-coal-rebalance", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "22 cc coal rebalance", "shorthand": "22-cc-coal-rebalance", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "ercot 23 stgas-drag-v1: ercot 22 base + gas_st_netload_drag=True (slope 0.00906, intercept -0.1376, cap 0.34)", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-23-stgas-drag-v1.js", "id": "2026-06-30-23-stgas-drag-v1", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "23 stgas drag v1", "shorthand": "23-stgas-drag-v1", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "ercot 24 ct-drag-v1: ercot 23 base + ct_netload_drag=True (slope 0.00703, intercept -0.1427, cap 0.47, ramp 15-22h) + CC_REGULAR econ_high delta -0.13 (was -0.10)", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-24-ct-drag-v1.js", "id": "2026-06-30-24-ct-drag-v1", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "24 ct drag v1", "shorthand": "24-ct-drag-v1", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "CAISO reliability-floor rebuild: per-(zone,class) day-gate + net-load CT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-caiso-41-relfloor-rebuild.js", "id": "2026-06-30-caiso-41-relfloor-rebuild", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 41 relfloor rebuild", "shorthand": "caiso-41-relfloor-rebuild", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "MISO reliability-floor rebuild: per-(zone,class) full-day day-gate (all miso23 levers retained)", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-miso-28-relfloor-rebuild.js", "id": "2026-06-30-miso-28-relfloor-rebuild", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso 28 relfloor rebuild", "shorthand": "miso-28-relfloor-rebuild", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "MISO 29: fixes 3+4 + fix 1 (miso_zonal_gas_basis) + fix 2 (cc_nameplate_summer_derate)", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-miso-29-zonal-gas.js", "id": "2026-06-30-miso-29-zonal-gas", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso 29 zonal gas", "shorthand": "miso-29-zonal-gas", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "MISO 30: coal sigmoid tuning (PRB sigmoid + BIT retune) + all run-29 flags restored", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-miso-30-cc-coal.js", "id": "2026-06-30-miso-30-cc-coal", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso 30 cc coal", "shorthand": "miso-30-cc-coal", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "neiso-41 = the neiso-39 keeper recipe re-solved with the rebuilt generic reliability-floor engine (RELIABILITY_FLOOR_REGISTRY) replacing the legacy per-ISO injector, with 3 enabled limbs: Boston CT_PEAKER hot, Central CC_REGULAR hot, Connecticut CT_PEAKER hot (full-day day-gate, no hour-of-day windows). Monthly LMP MAE is essentially unchanged vs keeper 39: 2023 $3.78->$3.77, 2024 $4.39->$4.41, 2025 $3.90->$3.90. Determination NOT-YET: the governance gate is unattested \u2014 no calibration_attestation.json is present in the bundle \u2014 so metrics are not the blocker.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-neiso-41-relfloor-rebuild.js", "id": "2026-06-30-neiso-41-relfloor-rebuild", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 41 relfloor rebuild", "shorthand": "neiso-41-relfloor-rebuild", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "NYISO reliability-floor rebuild (re-solve: floor enabled)", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-nyiso-36-relfloor-rebuild.js", "id": "2026-06-30-nyiso-36-relfloor-rebuild", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 36 relfloor rebuild", "shorthand": "nyiso-36-relfloor-rebuild", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "PJM reliability-floor rebuild: per-(zone,class) full-day day-gate. Builds on pjm 58 (seam border keeper) with the new generic registry-driven temperature reliability floor enabled. All (zone,class) limbs with enabled=True in reliability_floor_coeffs_PJM.csv are applied: CC_REGULAR, CT_PEAKER, CT_CHP, ST_GAS, COAL hot/cold per zone. Full-day step-gate (no HB windows), physically-grounded commit_frac x min_stable_pct floors. All other levers pjm 58 verbatim.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-pjm-65-relfloor-rebuild.js", "id": "2026-06-30-pjm-65-relfloor-rebuild", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 65 relfloor rebuild", "shorthand": "pjm-65-relfloor-rebuild", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "pjm 66 CC/CT calibration: two coupled levers for the C1 CC over-run / CT under-run. (1) CC_REGULAR committed 0.75\u21920.87 (Manual 15 SRMC floor, Rank 3 from pjm-cc-overgen-recommendation; ERCOT keeper 0.998). (2) CT_PEAKER dispatch fix from pjm-59/61 (committed 0.8784\u21921.25, econ_low 0.8792\u21921.05) so CTs clear as peakers not baseload \u2014 the pjm-58 keeper predates this structural correction. Also: CC econ_high 1.38\u21921.50 (steepen upper ramp for miss #2), ct_intermediate_split (threshold=30%), CC peak 5.0, cc_duct_peaking_cap_pct 18%, offer_curve_smoothing_mid=0.35 (pjm-61). All coal/hydro/congestion/seam-border levers pjm-58 verbatim. Energy-only, no P2 commitment.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-pjm-66-cc-ct.js", "id": "2026-06-30-pjm-66-cc-ct", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 66 cc ct", "shorthand": "pjm-66-cc-ct", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "pjm 67 reliability-floor + CC/CT calibration: merges pjm-65 (reliability_floor=True, per-(zone,class) temp-gated floors) with CC/CT offer-curve rebalance. CC_REGULAR committed 0.87\u21920.95 (Manual 15 SRMC, fix 2024 cheap-gas overrun), econ_low_share 0.50\u21920.55. CT split: 8 true peakers (committed 1.10, pct_peaking 20) + 62 intermediate CTs (committed 0.90, econ_low 0.92, pct_peaking 5) via ct_intermediate_split threshold=30%. CC econ_high 1.50, peak 5.0, cc_duct_peaking_cap_pct 18%, smoothing_mid 0.35. All coal/hydro/congestion/seam-border levers pjm-58 verbatim.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-pjm-67-relfloor-ccct.js", "id": "2026-06-30-pjm-67-relfloor-ccct", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 67 relfloor ccct", "shorthand": "pjm-67-relfloor-ccct", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "definition": "pjm 68 hydro backfill + interchange balance: layers on pjm-67 (reliability floor + CC/CT calibration). Hydro 2025 backfill: hydro_backfill_year=2024 restores 62 non-reporting EIA-923 hydro plants from 2024, EIA-930 monthly repin corrects budget level. BTM backfill: btm_backfill_year=2024 carries 2024 class totals for plants absent from the 2025 EIA-923 early release. All offer curves / coal / congestion / seam levers pjm-67 verbatim.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-30-pjm-68-hydro-backfill.js", "id": "2026-06-30-pjm-68-hydro-backfill", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 68 hydro backfill", "shorthand": "pjm-68-hydro-backfill", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "Re-registration of the ercot_ct_drag_v1 bundle (same solve as 2026-06-30-24-ct-drag-v1: ercot 23 base + ct_netload_drag=True, slope 0.00703/intercept -0.1427/cap 0.47/ramp 15-22h LST, + CC_REGULAR econ_high delta -0.13) with an updated dashboard payload after fixing scripts/render_calibration_html.py's ERCOT guard, which previously hard-excluded ERCOT from the C3c fallback tail-hour computation; C3c now falls back to model_price_by_zone when no ORDC overlay file exists for the year, so 2025 price-tail scores instead of SKIPPED (model 6h vs actual 31h > $200, ratio 0.19x). This surfaces a CAVEAT that was previously hidden, pushing the soft-caveat count to budget. Determination: NOT-YET (caveat budget exceeded: hard 2/1, soft 3/3) \u2014 hard caveats are C1 fuel-mix CC_REGULAR/CC_CHP classification split (2023/2024) and C2 gas system volume 2025 (-3.2%, EIA-923 preliminary vintage gap); soft caveats are C3b price-shape (2023/2024) and the newly-scored C3c price-tail (2025). All caveats are pre-existing, attested measured-input limitations, not new regressions.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-24-ct-drag-v1.js", "id": "2026-07-01-24-ct-drag-v1", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "24 ct drag v1", "shorthand": "24-ct-drag-v1", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "Adds corridor ATC forward envelope (PNW 0.43 / DSW 0.56 base fractions from measured p95 deliverability) and EIA-930 monthly hydro budget (fixes 2025 hydro gap 12\u219220 TWh). Carries all caiso-41 structural mechanisms: RA must-offer (min_load_frac 0.26), negative renewable offers, endogenous solar spill, CT net-load drag, gas hub basis overlay, reliability floor.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-caiso-42-atc-hydro.js", "id": "2026-07-01-caiso-42-atc-hydro", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 42 atc hydro", "shorthand": "caiso-42-atc-hydro", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle caiso43_gasfloor_probe.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-caiso-43-gasfloor-probe.js", "id": "2026-07-01-caiso-43-gasfloor-probe", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 43 gasfloor probe", "shorthand": "caiso-43-gasfloor-probe", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "(PROBE) Startup-cost-aware CAISO RA must-offer bridge: also holds a committed CC/CT at min-load across a midday gap LONGER than min-down when cycling is uneconomic (startup_per_mw > (MC-LMP)*min_load_frac*gap_hours; MC/LMP forward-derivable, no measured pin). Also fixes the RA bridge, which was inert on the binned CAISO fleet (tranches carry min_down=0). Mechanism works \u2014 holds +590 MW more spring-midday gas (2024), displaces imports, and makes 2024 negative prices appear (LMP min +23.2->-20.0) with monthly LMP toward actuals. Not promoted: annual gas inflates slightly (+0.3-0.8 TWh) on an already-over baseline, so the mix-vs-EIA-923 + no-inflation gate is not cleared. Keeper caiso-42 stands.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-caiso-44-startup-bridge.js", "id": "2026-07-01-caiso-44-startup-bridge", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 44 startup bridge", "shorthand": "caiso-44-startup-bridge", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "CAISO 45: startup bridge + bidir-intertie export sink (fix caiso-44 gas padding). Combines caiso-42 keeper (corridor ATC forward + EIA-930 hydro) with the caiso-44 startup-cost-aware RA bridge, plus caiso_bidir_intertie (export cap 3,500 MW from measured EIA-930 peak). The export leg lets the LP reverse the tie midday so surplus (committed gas at min-load + solar) displaces imports / sets sub-SRMC (negative) clearing prices instead of padding annual gas past EIA-923.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-caiso-45-export-sink.js", "id": "2026-07-01-caiso-45-export-sink", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 45 export sink", "shorthand": "caiso-45-export-sink", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "(PROBE) capdel A/B vs keeper caiso-42: byte-faithful replay of the keeper meta.json + capacity_deliverability_limits=True as the single delta. Part A live: the calibrated 7,500 MW WECC_import simultaneous-import cap is replaced by the published branch-group MIC sum crosswalked to the WECC_import seam (16,055 / 16,452 / 16,148 MW for 2023/24/25). ATTRIBUTION vs the same-code flag-off baseline (caiso 47 capdel-arm-a): 2023-only effect \u2014 the old cap is a hard ceiling (binds 744 h, p95 = max = 7,500); released, imports peak 11.2 GW (+0.69 TWh), CAISO-zone mean LMP 65.16 -> 64.73, and the model's entire 107-hour >$200 tail collapses to 0 h (actual 21 h): the calibrated cap, not physics, generated the 2023 scarcity tail \u2014 right tail-hour count in the keeper era came from an over-tight fitted scalar (rule #1: not a real mechanism). 2024/25 strict price no-op (max |dLMP| = 0.0; import tranches bind at 4.3-6.1 GW before either cap). Neither representation matches 2023 actuals (107 h = 5.1x vs 0 h = 0x of 21 h): the published MIC sum is never approached (slack >= 4.9 GW every hour) because summed per-branch-group MICs overstate simultaneous capability (parallel WECC paths; rule #12 boundary caveat) \u2014 the true binding representation lies between the tranche build and the MIC arithmetic sum. Never a keeper; flag stays default-off.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-caiso-46-capdel-probe.js", "id": "2026-07-01-caiso-46-capdel-probe", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 46 capdel probe", "shorthand": "caiso-46-capdel-probe", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "(PROBE baseline, arm A) Byte-faithful flag-OFF replay of keeper caiso-42 meta.json on current main \u2014 the drift-isolation baseline for caiso 46 capdel-probe. Same-code A/B result: the calibrated 7,500 MW cap is a hard ceiling in 2023 (p95 = max = 7,500; binds 744 h) and generates the model's ENTIRE 107-hour >$200 tail (actual 21 h) \u2014 identical to the registered keeper, so the caiso 46 tail collapse (107h -> 0h) is 100% the MIC seam-cap release, 0% main drift. 2024/25: prices byte-identical between arms (max |dLMP| = 0.0; import tranches bind at 4.3-6.1 GW before either cap), only cost-degenerate dispatch reshuffles. CAISO-zone mean LMP 2023: 65.16 (off) vs 64.73 (on). REPRODUCED independently at ef69fc1 (fresh container, full re-solve): the registered run payload regenerated byte-identical, dashboard payload deep-equal to keeper caiso-42 for all 3 years, with the 107 h tail, 744 h cap-binding and 4,312 / 6,076 MW 2024/25 import maxima confirmed exactly. Not a keeper.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-caiso-47-capdel-arm.js", "id": "2026-07-01-caiso-47-capdel-arm", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 47 capdel-arm-a", "shorthand": "caiso-47-capdel-arm", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "MISO 31: coal sigmoid retune \u2014 ceil<=1.0 for BIT/PRB/PRB_follower (coal cost doesnt follow gas; markup at dear gas was backwards)", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-miso-31-coal-sigmoid.js", "id": "2026-07-01-miso-31-coal-sigmoid", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso 31 coal sigmoid", "shorthand": "miso-31-coal-sigmoid", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "CT_PEAKER committed 1.55->1.15 + COAL_BIT sigmoid ceil 0.95->0.97 on miso23 keeper. Confirmed CT under-dispatch is structural (gmModel moved <0.2 TWh/year). C2 2025 gas improved -11.6%->-1.9% (PASS). NOT-YET: C1 CC/CT/ST substitution + C2 2025 coal +10.1% are model misses requiring reserve-demand co-opt + zonal granularity.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-miso-33-ct-committed.js", "id": "2026-07-01-miso-33-ct-committed", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso 33 ct committed", "shorthand": "miso-33-ct-committed", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "MISO 34 (PROBE, not keeper): energy+reserve co-opt ON (RBDC = MSSC + 400 MW regulating, $3500/MWh VOLL max penalty \u2014 MISO's real operating-reserve design). Reserve demand is NON-BINDING at 3-zone granularity: the ~1.7 GW market-wide requirement is trivially met by aggregate headroom, so it never forces commitment. CT_PEAKER stays inert (1.8/3.1/4.0 TWh, no forcing), ST_GAS flat (11.1/12.1/11.8), scarcity tail unchanged 0 h (max $61/$60/$73 vs actual 30/37/88 h). Price LEVEL lifted +2/+3/+4 $/MWh (legitimate reserve opportunity cost; C3a -14/-17% -> ~-8%), but coal 2025 REGRESSED to +18.7% (was +10.5% in miso-31) because reserve headroom pulled off gas is backfilled by coal. Diagnosis: the intended CT/ST-forcing + tail-lift needs sub-regional/zonal reserve zones with deliverability limits, NOT a market-wide curve at 3-zone copperplate and NOT a CC offer haircut. Next workstream: finer reserve-zone granularity.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-miso-34-reserve-coopt.js", "id": "2026-07-01-miso-34-reserve-coopt", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso 34 reserve coopt", "shorthand": "miso-34-reserve-coopt", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "NEISO run 42: cap CT_PEAKER peak 13.15\u21924.0 (ISO-NE offer cap, not ERCOT ORDC) and lower committed 1.55\u21921.35 (NYISO/CAISO-grounded start hurdle)", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-neiso-42-ct-peak.js", "id": "2026-07-01-neiso-42-ct-peak", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 42 ct peak", "shorthand": "neiso-42-ct-peak", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "neiso-43 closes NEISO: the neiso-41 keeper recipe (rebuilt generic reliability-floor engine, gas-hub-basis-daily overlay, EIA-930 hydro budget) plus a post-solve ORDC scarcity-price overlay with ISO-NE-grounded parameters (VOLL $2000 = Tariff III.1.10.1A offer cap, MCL 1200 MW = Millstone 3 largest contingency, sigma 900 MW = ISO-NE PAF Study 2022); the overlay adds a reserve-shortage adder to econ_prices for capacity economics but leaves dispatch/LP prices unchanged. Determination NOT-YET: caveat budget exceeded (hard 2/1, soft 3/3) \u2014 the hard caveats (C1 CC_REGULAR volume, C2 2025 gas +2.8%) both trace to the same EIA-930 gas-cell geothermal/biomass fold-in categorization, not a dispatch error, and the soft caveats (C3c price tail, C5b/C5c storage) trace to the LP's oil-parity cap on cold-hour prices, which the overlay prices for capacity value but does not lift out of the persisted LP prices that drive C3c/C5b/C5c; C6 governance passes.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-neiso-43-closeout.js", "id": "2026-07-01-neiso-43-closeout", "iso": "NEISO", "label": "neiso 43 closeout", "shorthand": "neiso-43-closeout", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle nyiso_unified_downstate.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-nyiso-37-unified-downstate.js", "id": "2026-07-01-nyiso-37-unified-downstate", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 37 unified downstate", "shorthand": "nyiso-37-unified-downstate", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "pjm 69 \u2014 PJM keeper (supersedes pjm-67). LIVE DELTA vs pjm-67 is the CC/CT offer-curve rebalance: CC_REGULAR committed 0.95->1.00 (shift CC rightward), CT_PEAKER committed 1.10->1.05 (let CTs compete earlier), targeting the 2024 CC_REGULAR overshoot / CT_PEAKER undershoot. pjm-69 CC_REGULAR/CT_PEAKER: 2024 331.4/17.0 TWh, 2025 321.6/29.7 TWh. SEAM CAPS INERT: pjm_seam_flow_limit + pjm_seam_export_limit are enabled but DO NOT BIND \u2014 2025 net export stayed +38.2 TWh vs actual +18.0 (target 18-22 unmet), interchange/prices ~unchanged from pjm-68. Root cause: the per-seam (month x hod) p90 export envelope, summed across the 5 neighbors, reconstitutes ~14.9 GW avg (~full 16.3 GW TTC) vs 2.1 GW actual net export, so frac~=1 and the export min_gen floor never binds (shared border zones AEP_Ohio / Dominion further inflate the sum). A per-seam marginal-p90 cap cannot bound PJM joint net position; the aggregate simultaneous-transfer-limit fix is deferred to pjm-70. All other settings pjm-68 verbatim.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-pjm-69-interchange-caps.js", "id": "2026-07-01-pjm-69-interchange-caps", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 69 cc/ct rebalance", "shorthand": "pjm-69-interchange-caps", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "PJM reserve-demand co-opt (unwind CC-over/CT-under + lift scarcity tail): layers energy+reserve co-optimization onto pjm-69 verbatim. energy_reserve_coopt=True turns on the co-optimized reserve balance row (measured PJM_RTO Primary requirement + published two-step ORDC $850/$300, Manual 11 sec 4.3.3 / FERC EL19-58). pjm_reserve_supply_cap=True caps cleared reserve at the fleet's physical 10-min deliverable ramp so the ORDC step can bind instead of drawing on idle full-fleet headroom (grounded capability, not a residual fit). Targets: lift C3c scarcity tail (0->6/18/59 actual) and unwind the CC-over/CT-under merit-order inversion by forcing reserve-eligible CCs to hold deliverable headroom. All other pjm-69 settings verbatim.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-pjm-70-reserve-coopt.js", "id": "2026-07-01-pjm-70-reserve-coopt", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 70 reserve coopt", "shorthand": "pjm-70-reserve-coopt", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "PJM 71 coal-bit-floor 0.65 (bracket bisect): floor=0.75 overshot (COAL_BIT -8/-13 TWh under). Bisecting the 0.55-0.75 bracket at 0.65 to find the floor that best splits C1-coal-volume and C3a-price. Sigmoid: floor=0.65, ceil=1.32, gas_mid=3.40, gas_slope=2.5. All other settings pjm-70 verbatim (reserve co-opt ON).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-pjm-71-coal-bit.js", "id": "2026-07-01-pjm-71-coal-bit", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 71 coal bit", "shorthand": "pjm-71-coal-bit", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "(PROBE) capdel A/B vs pjm-69 (the PJM keeper at probe time; since superseded by pjm 71 coal-bit): byte-faithful replay of the keeper meta.json + capacity_deliverability_limits=True as the single delta. CONFIRMED structural no-op in the PJM backcast: the Part A seam hook never fired (CETO/CETL are internal LDA limits \u2014 no seam import_limit resolves to PJM_external) and Part B gates only evolve_fleet, which the backcast never calls (fleet dispatched as observed). Residual deltas vs the REGISTERED keeper (e.g. 2023 CC_REGULAR -5.7 TWh, 2024 LMP -10.3% vs -10.1%) are main-branch code/data drift since the keeper solved at 4437acd, not the flag \u2014 the flag-off arm-A replay on current main (pjm 73 capdel-arm-a) reproduces this run byte-for-byte (max |dMW| = max |dLMP| = 0.0 over all ~24.9M dispatch rows, all 3 years). Forecast-facing Part B root-cause note: the curated PJM 'requirement' is the LDA CETO and 'import_limit' the CETL, so headroom = (in-zone firm + CETL) - CETO screens EVERY filed LDA long (+10 to +42 GW, 2026/27) by construction \u2014 the gate degenerates to a PJM-wide capacity-payment collapse (except unfiled West_APS), while PJM's real adequacy test is CETL >= CETO (tight for SWMAAC at 1.02x). The mechanism direction is right; the CETO-as-requirement semantic is misaligned to our zone-total headroom frame. Never a keeper; flag stays default-off.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-pjm-72-capdel-probe.js", "id": "2026-07-01-pjm-72-capdel-probe", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 72 capdel-probe", "shorthand": "pjm-72-capdel-probe", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-01", "definition": "(PROBE baseline, arm A) Byte-faithful flag-OFF replay of keeper pjm-69 meta.json on current main \u2014 the drift-isolation baseline for pjm 72 capdel-probe. Result: byte-identical to pjm 72 (max |dMW| = max |dLMP| = 0.0, all 3 years), proving capacity_deliverability_limits is a strict no-op in the PJM backcast; the residual deltas both runs share vs the registered pjm-69 keeper (2023 CC_REGULAR -5.7 TWh, 2024 LMP -10.3% vs -10.1%, storage discharge 4.70 vs 4.92 TWh) are main-branch code/data drift accumulated since pjm-69 solved at 4437acd. REPRODUCED independently at ef69fc1 (fresh container, full re-solve of both arms): the registered run payload regenerated byte-identical, and the same-sha flag-on/flag-off byte-diff re-verified at 0.0 across all 74,679,000 dispatch rows and 236,520 system rows \u2014 no further PJM drift f8b5dae through ef69fc1. Not a keeper.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-01-pjm-73-capdel-arm.js", "id": "2026-07-01-pjm-73-capdel-arm", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 73 capdel-arm-a", "shorthand": "pjm-73-capdel-arm", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-02", "definition": "ercot 25 chp-floor-v1 (KEEPER): ercot 24 ct-drag-v1 base + chp_export_floor_measured (CHP steam-following grid floor = measured EIA-923 class CF x sector grid-delivery share, superseding the pooled CAMPD p2 minimum) + the rule-#13 benchmark fix (bench-side BTM now measured-input: 923 class total x chp_btm_pct, never the model's own dispatch \u2014 the old circular bench could never fail C1 for CHP classes and drifted ~19 TWh between identical solves). CC_CHP passes C1 every year (2023 -1.22 / 2024 +0.81 / 2025 +0.07 TWh); NOT-YET at hard 2/1: C1 CC_REGULAR 2023 -8.56 TWh (root cause quantified: ~4.1 TWh missing nodal/GTC curtailment + ~3.7 TWh 923-vs-930 gas basis inconsistency landing on the marginal class) and C2 gas 2025 -2.8% (preliminary-923 vintage gap, improved from -3.2%). C3b 2024 newly passes (NRMSE 0.199).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-02-25-chp-floor-v1.js", "id": "2026-07-02-25-chp-floor-v1", "iso": "ERCOT", "label": "25 chp floor v1", "shorthand": "25-chp-floor-v1", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-02", "definition": "(PROBE \u2014 not promoted; caiso-42 keeper stands) caiso 48 solar-decommit bridge: caiso-45 (startup bridge + bidir-intertie export sink) plus the caiso_ra_bridge_decommit control \u2014 (1) economic bridges bounded to the 24-h day-ahead commitment horizon (CAISO IFM/RUC commit one operating day; multi-day idles are next-day decommit/re-offer decisions), and (2) gap hours where the candidate min-load floors exceed the P1 import-dispatch + export-sink absorption reprice to the curtailable-renewable keep-running offer, with uneconomic bridges decommitted cheapest-startup-first (RUC order). UC physics only; nothing fitted to a residual. KEY FINDING: refutes the caiso-45 open item \u2014 the bridge adds only +0.58/-0.88/+0.14 TWh of annual gas (P2\u2212P1, 2023/24/25); the '2025 +34.4% over EIA-923' guardrail trip was the P1 BASELINE against the raw preliminary 2025 vintage (55.2 TWh, ~71% reporting); on the repaired C2 basis 2025 gas is +7.9% (improved from caiso-42's +12.3%, still a HARD FAIL). Negatives/curtailment now appear (25/148/32 negative-price hours; 2024's 148 exist already at P1 via the export sink + negative offers). NOT promoted: C1 2024 CC_REGULAR widened to +11.10 TWh (caiso-42 +9.21) \u2014 the bidir baseline raises CC infra-marginality \u2014 and 2023 >$200 tail grew to 161h vs 21h (the WECC_import 7.5 GW simultaneous-cap thread). Next levers: P1 merit order (import tranche prices / CC offers), reconciled WECC seam limit (rule #12).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-02-caiso-48-solar-decommit.js", "id": "2026-07-02-caiso-48-solar-decommit", "iso": "CAISO", "label": "caiso 48 solar-decommit bridge", "shorthand": "caiso-48-solar-decommit", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-02", "definition": "MISO 35: 6-zone zonal refinement (phase 1 of the zonal-refinement scope) \u2014 Midwest split to the six measured EIA-930 sub-BA/LRZ-union zones (West/Plains/Illinois/Indiana/East/South) with per-season hourly CIL/CEL deliverability caps from the LOLE Study Reports and the RDT one-way pair (3,000/2,500 MW) attached to Plains (D3: no spurious Plains congestion; kept). One-way link bounds newly wired (the RDT had been silently symmetric). STRUCTURAL GATES: (1) interfaces bind \u2014 RDT N-to-S 71/326/1074 h and S-to-N 3077/2033/877 h (23/24/25); West CIL 701/232/672 h; Illinois CEL 1199/541/984 h; (2) zonal prices separate \u2014 hours with spread >$1 go 0 to 2519/1467/717 (max spread $20-28) \u2014 but zone MEAN LMPs stay nearly flat and South sits ~$0.4-1.1 below Midwest (S-to-N exports of cheap South CCGT; mean-level sign check needs the D6 per-hub LMP upload to score); (3) Illinois shows near-zero local scarcity as expected; East/South import-at-limit in scarcity hours cannot fire with the scarcity tail still at 0 h \u2014 reserve co-opt deliberately OFF this run (phase 2 re-enables it, gate 4). Same offer stack as the miso-31 keeper; level metrics essentially unchanged (2025 coal +10.4% vs +10.5%; C3a -8.5/-13.7/-17.0%). Most structurally faithful MISO run.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-02-miso-35-zonal-refinement.js", "id": "2026-07-02-miso-35-zonal-refinement", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso 35 zonal refinement", "shorthand": "miso-35-zonal-refinement", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-02", "definition": "MISO 36 (PROBE, wiring-bug evidence): miso-35 keeper flags + --energy-reserve-coopt, run BEFORE the run_year wiring fix. Demonstrates the flag was SILENTLY INERT for MISO on the backcast path: the co-opt dispatch-kwargs chain in run_calibration.run_year ended at NEISO, so the RBDC design (reserve_config._miso_design) was reachable only from the forecast runner. Results replicate miso-35 (energy-only LP; no reserve family, no reserve_price). Implication: miso-34's registered '+2/+3/+4 $/MWh reserve lift' was main-branch drift, not the co-opt \u2014 verified at miso-34's recorded clean sha 6712bdb (no MISO branch). Superseded by miso-37 (wired).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-02-miso-36-coopt-flag.js", "id": "2026-07-02-miso-36-coopt-flag", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso 36 coopt flag", "shorthand": "miso-36-coopt-flag", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-02", "definition": "MISO 37 (PROBE): market-wide RBDC energy+reserve co-opt genuinely IN the backcast LP for the first time (new run_year MISO branch -> unchanged _miso_design; requirement MSSC+400 MW = 4,353 MW, 8 ORDC steps $437-$3,500). Honest finding at 6-zone granularity: reserve clearing price EXACTLY $0 in all 26,280 hours \u2014 the ~4.4 GW market-wide requirement never binds against tens of GW of idle eligible headroom, and congestion does not change the market-wide sum (same result as the 3-zone miso3 probe and the PJM ORDC campaign). Dispatch differs from miso-36 only by degenerate-tie reshuffling (max LMP delta $0.29). Gates 1-3 unchanged from miso-35. Requirement NOT inflated, penalty NOT raised (rules #1/#11); structure retained. Tail lever moves to the zonal families (miso-38).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-02-miso-37-coopt-wired.js", "id": "2026-07-02-miso-37-coopt-wired", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso 37 coopt wired", "shorthand": "miso-37-coopt-wired", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-02", "definition": "MISO 38 (KEEPER): miso-37 + LOCATIONAL reserve family per scope \u00a76 (--miso-zonal-reserves): MISO-South zonal operating-reserve family, requirement = within-zone MSSC 3,953 MW (BPM-002 \u00a73.3.2 zonal minimum, the 'pre-determined largest zonal event'), priced at the PUBLISHED Zonal Operating Reserve Demand Curve (BPM-002 \u00a75.2.1.2 / Schedule 28-A: 20% @ $200, 70% @ $1,100, 10% @ $3,300 = VOLL - zonal reg proxy). Zero residual-fitted parameters. Gate 4 PARTIAL: the zonal family FIRES (reserve dual nonzero 266/287/875 h in 2023/24/25 \u2014 right year ordering, 2025-concentrated) but prices at re-dispatch opportunity cost ($8-21/MWh max), never at the curve steps: the LP always clears the requirement by re-dispatch, so the >$200 scarcity tail stays 0 h vs actual 30/37/88 and CT_PEAKER (5.0/8.4/8.8 TWh) / 2025 coal (212 TWh) are unchanged. Fit is unchanged vs miso-35 (mean LMP 28.65/26.06/34.74; duration-curve max lifts 54.5/62.0/70.4); keeper because strictly more structurally faithful (real co-opt + real zonal curve). Residual root cause is NOT missing reserve structure \u2014 perfect-foresight headroom; next levers: sub-shortage opportunity-cost band (per-gen ramp rows, memory-infeasible at plant scale) or the D6 per-hub LMP scoring once uploaded. GATE 2 SCORED vs D6 hub actuals (2026-07-02, docs/multi-iso/miso-zonal-gate2.md): PARTIAL/FAIL/PARTIAL. The South-below-Midwest sign is REAL (actual RT S-minus-Midwest -2.39/-3.20/-4.96 vs model -1.09/-0.55/-0.32) and RDT S->N binding hours carry real timing skill vs actual South-cheapest hours (lift 1.5-2.4x, precision to 92%) \u2014 but the five Midwest zone means are identical to the cent (pairwise mean-order agreement 10/5/5 of 15; the actual Indiana/East premium and the high-wind West discount, W-E -5 to -7, are absent), spread-duration is 1-2 orders under on every tier even vs DA, and the non-RDT CIL/CEL groups mostly bind DEGENERATELY (West CIL at cap 1152/238/661 h with $0 price effect). Root cause: LOLE island envelopes are adequacy-scale quantities, an order of magnitude above where real Midwest flowgates (MWEX, IN->MI, WUMS) price; next levers are published flowgate limits on L1-L6, the D1 Michigan split, and marginal-loss factors. Keeper standing unchanged (no more-faithful run exists; nothing tuned to the residual).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-02-miso-38-zonal-reserves.js", "id": "2026-07-02-miso-38-zonal-reserves", "iso": "MISO", "label": "miso 38 zonal reserves", "shorthand": "miso-38-zonal-reserves", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-02", "definition": "(PROBE, NOT-YET) Keeper-34 migration onto the unified registry-driven reliability-floor engine, now with a new CONTINUOUS temperature-ramp family (inject_reliability_floor interpolates (threshold, floor_pct) knots) that faithfully reproduces the legacy downstate CT/ST floors' base+slope(T-T0) commitment curve instead of an over-firing step. Config = keeper-34 exact (offer curve, gas_hub_basis_daily+overlay, zonal gas basis, local-selfsupply/firm-imports/import-recon, energy-reserve co-opt). Still FAILS C1: 2024 ST_GAS -1.94 TWh (band +/-1.35), 2023 CC_REGULAR +2.61, 2023 ST_GAS -1.30. Root cause is NOT the floor mechanism (the floor forces the same energy) but an economic merit-order shift since keeper-34's unrecoverable dirty sha: Capital_Hudson ST (base_24h=0 in the data, so purely economic) is squeezed to 0.30 TWh vs ~1.2 in keeper 34. Closing it would need a CH persistent base ungrounded in data (rule #9/#11) or offer-curve residual-tuning (forbidden for a do-no-harm migration). The continuous-ramp engine stays as correct structure (rule #1) regardless of the fit.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-02-nyiso-37-unified-ct.js", "id": "2026-07-02-nyiso-37-unified-ct", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 38 unified-ct-ramp (PROBE)", "shorthand": "nyiso-37-unified-ct", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-02", "definition": "nyiso-39 closes the keeper-34 -> unified-engine migration: the nyiso-38 recipe (keeper-34-exact offer curve, gas-hub-basis daily+overlay, zonal gas basis, local-selfsupply, energy+reserve co-opt, continuous temperature-ramp reliability floor) plus --priced-interchange, the keeper-34 flag the migration template had silently dropped. Keeper 34's run_config records priced_interchange=true; without it --nyiso-firm-imports and --nyiso-import-reconciliation are hard-gated OFF, so the nyiso-36/37/38 probes silently served the measured EIA-930 schedule as a fixed demand-side subtraction instead of the priced import node + HQ firm floor + monthly reconciliation band - the fixed schedule displaced the un-floored mid-merit downstate steam (the misdiagnosed 'Capital_Hudson economic squeeze'; keeper 34's own model CH ST was 0.16 TWh, not the ~1.2 the handoff claimed - that figure is the CAMPD actual keeper 34 equally under-ran). Restoring the flag clears every hard criterion: 2024 ST_GAS 8.89 -> 9.41 TWh (-1.40 vs rubric band +/-1.50), 2023 CC_REGULAR +2.61 -> +1.06, 2023 ST_GAS -1.30 -> -0.86; C2/C4/C5a hold. Migration fidelity verified value-by-value against keeper sha 180d579 (interchange + reserve unifications byte-faithful for NYISO); one post-keeper measured addition kept per rule #10 (4,350 MW Gold Book simultaneous-import limit). Soft C3a/b/c price caveats persist (ledgered ORDC/reserve-scarcity frontier): 2024/2025 land at keeper-34 levels (C3a -19.2/-13.2% vs -19.4/-13.7%), while 2023 runs deeper (C3a -15.6% vs keeper 34's -8.0%) because the priced-import node's elastic imports soften off-peak prices further than the fixed measured-schedule probe. Verdict CALIBRATED-WITH-CAVEATS (3 documented caveats); promoted NYISO keeper, replacing the non-reproducible nyiso-34 (legacy floor injectors deleted, solve sha dirty/unrecorded).", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-02-nyiso-39-priced-interchange.js", "id": "2026-07-02-nyiso-39-priced-interchange", "iso": "NYISO", "label": "nyiso 39 priced interchange", "shorthand": "nyiso-39-priced-interchange", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-02", "definition": "PJM 74 CC/CT offer rebalance (planned as run-72): one Manual-15-grounded step along the pjm-69 axis. CC_REGULAR econ_low 0.92->1.00 (composite-cost floor: LP has no no-load variable, M15 cost recovery puts every increment >= 1.0x full-load AHR fuel). CT_PEAKER econ_high 1.40->1.27 (M15 Sec 2.3 10% cost adder cap on worst-tranche IHR ~1.15x -> ceiling ~1.27; 1.40 exceeded the cost cap). CC committed stays 1.00 (at/past M15 min-load floor; committed raises are year-uniform and would break clean 2023 CC); CT peak block untouched (scarcity/tail out of scope per pjm-reserve-ordc Phase 1-2 gate). All else pjm-71 verbatim (coal_bit floor 0.65, reserve co-opt ON). RESULT: PJM keeper (supersedes pjm-71; most structurally faithful \u2014 two uncited offer values replaced by M15-derived ones). CC down ~-4.4 TWh ALL years (year-uniform), CT +1.0..+1.8: 2024 CC_REGULAR +20.9->+16.6 TWh, CT_PEAKER -9.7->-8.8 (both still C1 FAIL), 2025 CC +4.9->+0.3; 2023 CC -2.3->-6.7 (NEW C1 volume fail \u2014 the cost of removing the below-cost band). Prices all improved: C3a 2024 -9.4->-8.7%, 2025 -14.8->-14.1%, C3b 2025 0.208->0.204, C2 2025 gas +4.1->+3.4% (coal +2.6->+3.4%). C6 PASS (attested). Verdict NOT-YET. The 2024-concentrated CC-over/CT-under residual is NOT reachable by year-uniform offer multipliers \u2014 root cause is the missing commitment tightening / per-gen reserve structure (pjm-reserve-ordc Phases 1-2); offer retune along this axis is exhausted.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-02-pjm-74-cc-ct.js", "id": "2026-07-02-pjm-74-cc-ct", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 74 cc ct rebalance", "shorthand": "pjm-74-cc-ct", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, {"date": "2026-07-02", "definition": "PJM 75 CT net-load deployment drag + CC demonstrated-peak cap. CT_PEAKER min-gen floor clip(0.01108*netGW - 0.9987, 0, 0.46) in ramp h[15,22) \u2014 measured CAMPD CT CF vs EIA-930 net-load (derive_pjm_ct_netload_drag.py), the ERCOT/CAISO keeper mechanism with PJM-fit hinge coefficients; forward-native replacement for the diagnosed AS/RUC deployment energy. CC capacity capped at CAMPD demonstrated sustained max (p99.9, 2023-25) for 17 plants whose nameplate exceeds it >1.1x (-2686 MW phantom top-band; derive_cc_capacity_reconcile.py --iso PJM --mode cap, EIA-923 feasibility-guarded). All else pjm-74 verbatim (M15 offer moves, coal_bit floor 0.65, reserve co-opt ON, commitment=False). RESULT (NOT-YET, new PJM keeper): both CT_PEAKER C1 fails cleared (2024 -8.76 -> -4.48 TWh now in band; 2023 +2.49 in band); 2024 CC_REGULAR halved +16.57 -> +8.85 TWh/+0.8pp (still FAIL); 2023 CC -6.71 -> -14.54 TWh (accepted cost: the cap removes phantom capacity the 2023 fit leaned on); C2 2025 gas +3.4 -> +2.7%, coal +3.4 -> +4.2%; C3a 2024 -8.7 -> -8.1%, 2025 -14.1 -> -13.4%; C3b/C4/C5a/C6 PASS; C3c 0 tail hours unchanged (blocked on per-gen reserve thread). Deltas vs pjm-74 near-uniform (CC -7.5..-7.9, CT +4.0..+4.5 TWh/yr): the floor overlaps hours the LP already dispatched, so the year-differentiation is milder than the floor energy (4.7/6.3/7.2 TWh) implied.", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-07-02-pjm-75-ct-drag.js", "id": "2026-07-02-pjm-75-ct-drag", "iso": "PJM", "label": "pjm 75 ct-drag cc-cap", "shorthand": "pjm-75-ct-drag", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}];